---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../config/site";
import { breadcrumbJsonLd, personJsonLd } from "../lib/seo";

const title = "About John Bowskill — Margaret River";
const description =
  "John Bowskill is a practical generalist in the Margaret River region of Western Australia, working across software, electronics, photography, farming and exhibition technology.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/about"
  image="/images/john-bowskill.jpg"
  imageAlt="Portrait of John Bowskill in the Margaret River region"
  jsonLd={[
    personJsonLd(),
    breadcrumbJsonLd([
      { name: "Home", path: "/" },
      { name: "About", path: "/about" },
    ]),
  ]}
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="section-kicker">About</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">The common thread isn’t the industry. It’s solving the problem.</h1>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="wrap about-layout">
      <figure class="portrait">
        <img
          src="/images/john-bowskill.jpg"
          alt="John Bowskill standing outdoors in the Margaret River region"
          width="800"
          height="705"
        />
        <figcaption>John Bowskill, Margaret River region, Western Australia.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I live near Margaret River, on a property that has been a group settlement block,
          a dairy, an organic winery and now a shared family farm. I run an aquaponics
          greenhouse there. I also write software, build electronics, take photographs, and
          occasionally construct rooms that project images onto four walls at once.
        </p>
        <p>
          That list looks scattered until you watch what actually happens. A farm needs
          water-quality data, so sensors appear. A veterinary workflow needs structured
          reports, so an application appears. A shipwreck on a local beach turns into years
          of research, then an exhibition, then the software required to run the
          exhibition. The job title keeps changing. The habit doesn’t.
        </p>
        <p>
          I have a long history of solving practical problems, including years of
          veterinary work. That background is useful. It teaches you to look carefully,
          not to invent a more complicated story than the evidence supports, and to build
          tools around the way people already work rather than the way a product manager
          might prefer them to work.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>I usually start building because an appropriate existing product doesn’t exist.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          Curiosity is the other constant. Arduino and Raspberry Pi boards, wireless
          sensor networks, AI-assisted development, photogrammetry, Unreal Engine,
          MetaHumans, drones, underwater cameras — I pick these up because a particular
          problem has made them necessary, then keep going when they turn out to be
          interesting in their own right.
        </p>
        <p>
          I am not trying to be a specialist in one narrow profession. Breadth is the
          point, as long as it stays attached to a real constraint: a greenhouse that
          has to stay alive, a report that a colleague has to finish, a visitor standing
          in a dark room who should understand something they didn’t understand when they
          walked in.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want the work rather than the biography, start with
          <a href="/projects">the projects</a>. The flagship piece at the moment is
          <a href="/projects/ss-georgette-150th">The Georgette 150th</a>, which lives at
          <a href="https://exhibition.margies.app" rel="noopener noreferrer">exhibition.margies.app</a>.
          The rest of this site — {site.shortName} — is the root for everything else.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/contact">Get in touch</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
